1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">What is actually essential for offline
reinforcement learning done via supervised learning?</font>

2
00:00:04,310 --> 00:00:08,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">This ICLR 2022 paper by Scott Emmons, Benjamin
Eysenbach, Ilya Kostrikov,</font>

3
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:11,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">and Sergey Levine strips reinforcement learning via
supervised learning, or RvS,</font>

4
00:00:11,930 --> 00:00:15,660
<font color="#FFFFFF">down to its bare essentials.</font>

5
00:00:15,740 --> 00:00:19,470
<font color="#FFFFFF">Their surprising finding is that a plain two-layer
feedforward network,</font>

6
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:23,280
<font color="#FFFFFF">trained simply to maximize likelihood,</font>

7
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,090
<font color="#FFFFFF">matches state-of-the-art results from far more
complex methods built on temporal-difference</font>

8
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:30,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">learning or on Transformer sequence models.</font>

9
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:34,710
<font color="#FFFFFF">The two things that really matter, they show,</font>

10
00:00:34,790 --> 00:00:38,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">are choosing the model's capacity carefully and
choosing what to condition on,</font>

11
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:42,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">goals or rewards.</font>

12
00:00:42,410 --> 00:00:46,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">Everything else turns out to be optional.</font>

13
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:49,563
<font color="#FFFFFF">Recent work showed that plain supervised learning,</font>

14
00:00:49,643 --> 00:00:52,623
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no temporal-difference bootstrapping at all,</font>

15
00:00:52,703 --> 00:00:56,558
<font color="#FFFFFF">can be remarkably effective for offline
reinforcement learning.</font>

16
00:00:56,638 --> 00:00:58,182
<font color="#FFFFFF">But the picture was muddy.</font>

17
00:00:58,262 --> 00:01:03,803
<font color="#FFFFFF">Different papers reached contradictory conclusions
about what actually makes these methods</font>

18
00:01:03,883 --> 00:01:06,426
<font color="#FFFFFF">work: some emphasized advantage weighting,</font>

19
00:01:06,506 --> 00:01:09,736
<font color="#FFFFFF">others reached for large Transformer sequence models.</font>

20
00:01:09,816 --> 00:01:13,859
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core question this paper asks is simple, yet
still unanswered.</font>

21
00:01:13,939 --> 00:01:17,544
<font color="#FFFFFF">When does supervised learning for offline RL
actually work,</font>

22
00:01:17,624 --> 00:01:23,102
<font color="#FFFFFF">and which of its many algorithmic components are
truly essential versus merely incidental</font>

23
00:01:23,182 --> 00:01:26,350
<font color="#FFFFFF">complexity that could be stripped away without cost?</font>

24
00:01:26,730 --> 00:01:30,097
<font color="#FFFFFF">Value-based methods dominate offline and off-policy RL,</font>

25
00:01:30,177 --> 00:01:33,356
<font color="#FFFFFF">and they come with appealing theoretical guarantees.</font>

26
00:01:33,436 --> 00:01:36,113
<font color="#FFFFFF">But in practice they are difficult to apply.</font>

27
00:01:36,193 --> 00:01:42,694
<font color="#FFFFFF">They require complex tricks to stabilize learning
and careful tuning of many interacting hyperparameters.</font>

28
00:01:42,774 --> 00:01:48,585
<font color="#FFFFFF">An attractive alternative is to convert the
reinforcement learning problem into a conditional,</font>

29
00:01:48,665 --> 00:01:51,656
<font color="#FFFFFF">filtered, or weighted imitation learning problem,</font>

30
00:01:51,736 --> 00:01:57,484
<font color="#FFFFFF">using the insight that experience that is
suboptimal for one task may be optimal for another.</font>

31
00:01:57,564 --> 00:02:02,185
<font color="#FFFFFF">If a minimal supervised recipe can match these
complex value-based methods,</font>

32
00:02:02,265 --> 00:02:05,506
<font color="#FFFFFF">it would give practitioners a dependable field guide,</font>

33
00:02:05,586 --> 00:02:10,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it would also reveal exactly where such
supervised methods still break down.</font>

34
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:14,935
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three contributions.</font>

35
00:02:15,015 --> 00:02:19,049
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it does not propose a brand-new algorithm;</font>

36
00:02:19,129 --> 00:02:23,164
<font color="#FFFFFF">instead it places many existing goal-conditioned
and reward-conditioned methods under one common framework,</font>

37
00:02:23,244 --> 00:02:27,278
<font color="#FFFFFF">which the authors call RvS, reinforcement learning
via supervised learning.</font>

38
00:02:27,358 --> 00:02:31,393
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

39
00:02:31,473 --> 00:02:35,507
<font color="#FFFFFF">through extensive experiments it boils these
methods down to their essential elements,</font>

40
00:02:35,587 --> 00:02:39,622
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing that a two-layer feedforward network
trained to maximize likelihood is competitive</font>

41
00:02:39,702 --> 00:02:43,736
<font color="#FFFFFF">with far more complex state-of-the-art methods.</font>

42
00:02:43,816 --> 00:02:47,851
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, it identifies exactly which design choices
matter, namely model capacity,</font>

43
00:02:47,931 --> 00:02:51,965
<font color="#FFFFFF">regularization, and what you condition on, and it
honestly probes the limits,</font>

44
00:02:52,045 --> 00:02:56,080
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing that RvS is comparatively weak on purely
random data.</font>

45
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:58,544
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method is deliberately simple.</font>

46
00:02:58,624 --> 00:03:04,655
<font color="#FFFFFF">RvS assumes an agent in a Markov decision process
and trains a policy conditioned on an outcome,</font>

47
00:03:04,735 --> 00:03:08,983
<font color="#FFFFFF">which can be either a future goal state or an
average future return.</font>

48
00:03:09,063 --> 00:03:11,593
<font color="#FFFFFF">Given an offline dataset of trajectories,</font>

49
00:03:11,673 --> 00:03:17,386
<font color="#FFFFFF">it applies hindsight relabeling: every observed
action becomes a demonstration for whatever</font>

50
00:03:17,466 --> 00:03:20,950
<font color="#FFFFFF">outcome actually occurred later in that same trajectory.</font>

51
00:03:21,030 --> 00:03:26,934
<font color="#FFFFFF">The policy itself is just a feedforward multilayer
perceptron with two fully connected layers,</font>

52
00:03:27,014 --> 00:03:31,644
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the outcome is fed in simply by concatenating
it onto the input state.</font>

53
00:03:31,724 --> 00:03:37,755
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training then maximizes the log-likelihood of the
observed actions under the conditioned policy.</font>

54
00:03:37,835 --> 00:03:42,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">There is no advantage weighting, no
temporal-difference bootstrapping,</font>

55
00:03:42,290 --> 00:03:47,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">and no Transformer, only a maximum-likelihood
objective over relabeled experience.</font>

56
00:03:47,810 --> 00:03:50,215
<font color="#FFFFFF">The evaluation is deliberately broad.</font>

57
00:03:50,295 --> 00:03:54,311
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the D4RL benchmark, the authors use three
suites: AntMaze,</font>

58
00:03:54,391 --> 00:03:59,080
<font color="#FFFFFF">which requires an eight-degree-of-freedom quadruped
to navigate a maze;</font>

59
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:04,116
<font color="#FFFFFF">Gym Locomotion, with HalfCheetah, Hopper, and
Walker across random, medium,</font>

60
00:04:04,196 --> 00:04:08,280
<font color="#FFFFFF">medium-replay, and medium-expert datasets; and
Franka Kitchen,</font>

61
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:13,317
<font color="#FFFFFF">a nine-degree-of-freedom manipulation task built
from human demonstrations.</font>

62
00:04:13,397 --> 00:04:17,011
<font color="#FFFFFF">They also use the GCSL suite of goal-conditioned tasks,</font>

63
00:04:17,091 --> 00:04:21,779
<font color="#FFFFFF">including two-dimensional navigation, Sawyer arm
control, Lunar Lander,</font>

64
00:04:21,859 --> 00:04:23,055
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a robotic claw,</font>

65
00:04:23,135 --> 00:04:27,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">which they adapt for offline RL by collecting data
with a random policy.</font>

66
00:04:27,970 --> 00:04:34,270
<font color="#FFFFFF">All scores are normalized into a
zero-to-one-hundred range so methods can be compared directly.</font>

67
00:04:34,650 --> 00:04:36,812
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is striking.</font>

68
00:04:36,892 --> 00:04:42,556
<font color="#FFFFFF">Using nothing but a two-layer feedforward network
trained with maximum likelihood,</font>

69
00:04:42,636 --> 00:04:46,970
<font color="#FFFFFF">RvS reaches state-of-the-art performance across
several suites.</font>

70
00:04:47,050 --> 00:04:52,154
<font color="#FFFFFF">On AntMaze, goal-conditioned RvS scores fifty-three
point five on average,</font>

71
00:04:52,234 --> 00:04:56,357
<font color="#FFFFFF">edging out the best value-based baseline at fifty
point six.</font>

72
00:04:56,437 --> 00:04:59,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Franka Kitchen it reaches fifty-four.</font>

73
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:05,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the GCSL suite it scores sixty-two, beating the
online GCSL method at fifty-eight,</font>

74
00:05:05,194 --> 00:05:07,356
<font color="#FFFFFF">despite using only offline data.</font>

75
00:05:07,436 --> 00:05:08,897
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on Gym Locomotion,</font>

76
00:05:08,977 --> 00:05:15,483
<font color="#FFFFFF">reward-conditioned RvS matches Decision Transformer
while using a simple multilayer perceptron</font>

77
00:05:15,563 --> 00:05:17,654
<font color="#FFFFFF">instead of a large Transformer.</font>

78
00:05:17,734 --> 00:05:22,418
<font color="#FFFFFF">Even on stitching tasks, long thought to demand
dynamic programming,</font>

79
00:05:22,498 --> 00:05:24,660
<font color="#FFFFFF">goal-conditioned RvS keeps pace.</font>

80
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:28,938
<font color="#FFFFFF">The ablations pin down what actually matters.</font>

81
00:05:29,018 --> 00:05:32,917
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

82
00:05:32,997 --> 00:05:36,895
<font color="#FFFFFF">capacity: the best architectures are notably larger
than those used in standard online RL or imitation learning,</font>

83
00:05:36,975 --> 00:05:40,874
<font color="#FFFFFF">and widening the network up to about a thousand
hidden units generally helps.</font>

84
00:05:40,954 --> 00:05:44,852
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, regularization: dropout is not universally good.</font>

85
00:05:44,932 --> 00:05:48,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">It boosts performance on the small,
human-demonstration kitchen-complete dataset,</font>

86
00:05:48,911 --> 00:05:52,809
<font color="#FFFFFF">but it hurts on hopper-medium-expert and on
antmaze-medium-play.</font>

87
00:05:52,889 --> 00:05:56,788
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

88
00:05:56,868 --> 00:06:00,766
<font color="#FFFFFF">the output distribution: a categorical distribution
over discretized actions matches or</font>

89
00:06:00,846 --> 00:06:04,745
<font color="#FFFFFF">beats a unimodal Gaussian across the GCSL tasks,</font>

90
00:06:04,825 --> 00:06:08,723
<font color="#FFFFFF">which fits the broader theme that more policy
capacity helps.</font>

91
00:06:08,803 --> 00:06:12,702
<font color="#FFFFFF">Finally, validation loss correlates only loosely
with final performance,</font>

92
00:06:12,782 --> 00:06:16,680
<font color="#FFFFFF">so it is not a reliable tuning signal on its own.</font>

93
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:20,612
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is essentially a practitioner's field guide.</font>

94
00:06:20,692 --> 00:06:26,538
<font color="#FFFFFF">You do not need advantage weighting or a
Transformer to do offline RL by supervised learning.</font>

95
00:06:26,618 --> 00:06:31,444
<font color="#FFFFFF">A plain two-layer feedforward network, trained
simply to maximize likelihood,</font>

96
00:06:31,524 --> 00:06:34,057
<font color="#FFFFFF">is competitive with the state of the art,</font>

97
00:06:34,137 --> 00:06:40,174
<font color="#FFFFFF">as long as you get two things right: carefully tune
the model's capacity and its regularization,</font>

98
00:06:40,254 --> 00:06:44,061
<font color="#FFFFFF">and choose the right thing to condition on, goals
or rewards.</font>

99
00:06:44,141 --> 00:06:48,968
<font color="#FFFFFF">The concrete recipe is to grow the network width
until performance saturates,</font>

100
00:06:49,048 --> 00:06:50,624
<font color="#FFFFFF">then add a little dropout.</font>

101
00:06:50,704 --> 00:06:53,683
<font color="#FFFFFF">The honest caveat is that on purely random data,</font>

102
00:06:53,763 --> 00:06:56,104
<font color="#FFFFFF">temporal-difference methods still win,</font>

103
00:06:56,184 --> 00:06:59,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">which the authors flag as an open problem for future work.</font>
